<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#rye">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rye</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rye is a hardy cereal grain that grows well in cool climates and poorer soils, long a staple of northern and eastern European agriculture. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> exported large quantities of rye and wheat through the Baltic port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. According to the Commonwealth profile, this grain trade enriched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szlachta">szlachta</a> nobility and encouraged the tightening of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>.</p></div>
